---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { COUNCILS } from '../../lib/councils';
import { P } from '../../lib/engine/params';
import { BANK_SOURCE } from '../../lib/engine/calendar';
const lang = 'en';
const all = COUNCILS.flatMap((c) => Object.values(c.years).filter((y) => y && y.status !== 'not-published' && y.source));
const byVia = (v: string) => all.filter((y) => y!.source!.via === v).length;
const byFmt = (f: string) => all.filter((y) => y!.source!.format === f).length;
const s = (k: keyof typeof P.sources) => P.sources[k];
---
<PageLayout title="Term Dates Method: How Each Council Calendar Is Checked" description="How When Is Half Term reads each council's term dates, checks them by program, counts school days, bank holidays and INSET days, and what the tools cannot know." lang={lang} jsonLd={[articleSchema({ title: 'How the term dates are read and tested', description: 'Method of When Is Half Term', path: route('method', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path: route('method', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><p class="kicker">Method</p><h1 class="mt-1 text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">How the dates are read, checked and counted</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2>1. One council, one official page</h2>
    <p>For each of the {COUNCILS.length} calendars on the site we open the council's own term dates page and the file it links to. Of the {all.length} school years stored, {byFmt('pdf')} were read in a PDF, {byFmt('html')} on a web page and {byFmt('doc') + byFmt('docx')} in a Word document. {byVia('wayback')} came from the Internet Archive copy of the official file, because the council's website turns away automated readers; {byVia('webfetch')} were opened through a browser reader. The address of the official file and the reading date are stored with every year and printed on the council page.</p>
    <p>Northern Ireland has no councils setting term dates: we use the common closure dates published by the <a href={s('ni_holidays').url} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">{s('ni_holidays').label.en}</a> and explain what each school still decides.</p>
    <h2>2. What is stored</h2>
    <p>Each calendar becomes a list of <em>sessions</em>, the stretches of weeks when the school is in term, and of named <em>holidays</em> between them. In-service days fixed by a council (common in Scotland and Wales) are stored as dates when pupils are at home. A bank holiday inside a term, such as the early May bank holiday in England, does not end a term but closes the school that day.</p>
    <h2>3. The checks a calendar must pass</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>no term starts or ends on a Saturday or Sunday;</li>
      <li>two terms never overlap and come in date order;</li>
      <li>every weekday between two terms belongs to a named holiday, so no day is left "open" by mistake;</li>
      <li>no in-service day falls outside term;</li>
      <li>the number of school days is printed and compared with what the council states when it states one.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Then we compare each file with the text kept from the council page, line by line. The checks found real slips: Kent's 2026-27 PDF lists the May Day bank holiday as "3/05/2026" when it means 3 May 2027.</p>
    <h2>4. Is school open on a given day?</h2>
    <p>The engine answers in this order: weekend; a named holiday; an in-service day fixed by the council; another closure listed by the council; a bank holiday (England, Wales and Northern Ireland, from the <a href={BANK_SOURCE.url} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">GOV.UK bank holidays feed</a>, read on {displayDate(BANK_SOURCE.retrieved, 'en-GB')}); otherwise a school day. In Scotland, councils list their own public and local holidays, which do not follow the bank holiday list, so only the council's list counts.</p>
    <h2>5. School days, countdowns and comparisons</h2>
    <p>"School days" are the days the engine marks open between two dates, both included. A countdown gives calendar days to the first day of the next break and school days until then. A comparison walks through every weekday of the year from 1 September and counts the days when both councils are off, and the days when only one is. In England the law asks for at least {P.schoolYear.englandSessions} sessions, two a day, so {P.schoolYear.englandPupilDays} days for pupils (<a href={s('school_day_regs').url} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">{s('school_day_regs').label.en}</a>), while teachers work {P.schoolYear.englandTeacherDays} days: councils that publish 195 days leave five for each school to use as INSET days.</p>
    <h2>6. Absence penalties</h2>
    <p>The fine calculator applies the national framework of the <a href={s('dfe_attendance').url} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Department for Education attendance guidance</a> for England and regulation 4 of the <a href={s('wales_penalty_regs').url} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Education (Penalty Notices) (Wales) Regulations 2013</a>. Amounts are kept in one parameter file read on {displayDate(P.retrieved_at, 'en-GB')}.</p>
    <h2>7. What the tools cannot know</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>the INSET days an English school picks for itself, and any day a school closes for a polling station, a strike or the weather;</li>
      <li>the dates of academies, free schools, voluntary aided, foundation and independent schools, which can differ from the council's;</li>
      <li>a change a council makes after our reading date: the date is on every page, and we re-read calendars each term.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Method version: {LAST_UPDATED}. Questions: <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact</a>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
